course: dictionary
lesson: 8418
title: Rural: the countryside
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Rural means to do with the countryside, not towns or cities. When you read it, picture fields, farms and villages.
explain: «He is cut off in the middle of how very clev—— when he sees a chauffeur in leather gaiters and two large rural policemen running after him, about two fields away.» The words fields and running show that this happens in open country, so rural must mean country. The sentence even tells you so in brackets: (country).
explain: Rural is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in rural area, rural life or rural school. Its opposite is urban, which means to do with towns and cities.
fact: Rural means to do with the countryside, not towns or cities.
fact: A rural area often has farms, villages and fewer people.
fact: Urban means to do with towns and cities, so it is the opposite of rural.
fact: You can say rural area, rural life or rural school.
fact: Rural schools may be small, because fewer children live in the countryside.
exercise: «He is cut off ... two large rural (country) policemen running after him, about two fields away.» What does rural mean here? | Relating to a big city | Made of wood | *Relating to the countryside | Very fast
exercise: Which phrase would you use for a village with farms and fields? | urban rush | barely school | ancient ship | *rural area
exercise: What is the opposite of rural? | Glum | Barely | *Urban | Ancient
test: Which word means to do with towns and cities? | rural | luminous | *urban | glum
test: A small school in the hills with few pupils is a ___ school. | curt | urban | *rural | famished
exam: «first, of stealing a valuable motor-car; secondly, of driving to the public danger; and, thirdly, of gross impertinence to the rural police» What does rural police mean here? | *Police who work in the countryside | Police who work only in cities | Police who give rude cheek | Police who drive motor-cars
